Commit 5365acc567 ("libio: Convert __obstack_vprintf_internal to
buffers (bug 27124)") moved obstack formatting to printf_buffer.  The
flush callback only requests room for the pending character, so a
large ready-to-copy write can repeatedly grow and copy the object.

When a byte-oriented obstack buffer is full, reserve space for the
remaining write and any pending character at once.  Lengths below
INT_MAX use this path; larger writes retain the existing path.  The
static reference is weak so ordinary static printf users do not pull
in obstack code.  Wide-character output and obstack growth policy are
unchanged.

The new shared and static tests cover printf/vprintf, buffer boundaries,
%n, embedded NUL, append operations, conversion failure, and custom
printf handlers.  For a 1 MiB string starting with a 128-byte obstack
chunk, the allocation count falls from 61 to 2, including the initial
chunk.  Add bench-obprintf for fresh and preallocated obstacks.

Measurements on x86_64 with GCC 16.2.1, identical build configuration,
the same benchmark executable and loader, and alternating baseline /
patched libc runs pinned to one CPU (medians of nine runs each):

                              baseline       patched
  fresh obstack, 64 KiB        202684         11093.7
  fresh obstack, 1 MiB         4570660        278504
  preallocated, 4 KiB          230.206        240.257
  preallocated, 1 MiB          131870         136137

These are hp_timing units, not nanoseconds.  The fresh-obstack cases
improve by 18.27x and 16.41x, while the preallocated cases are 4.37% and
3.24% slower.  An earlier seven-pair run showed the same direction,
including a 6.35% slowdown for preallocated 4 KiB output.  The machine
was not isolated from other activity; the preallocated regressions
remain unexplained.  This is not a claim of a general printf speedup.

The following tests passed on x86_64-linux-gnu:
  stdio-common/tst-obprintf
  stdio-common/tst-obprintf-large
  stdio-common/tst-obprintf-large-static
  libio/tst-stdio-static
  debug/tst-fortify-c-default-2-def

With the implementation change removed, the new test reports 18
allocation-count failures; content and length checks still pass.
A clean full-suite result is not available.  The limited stdio-common
run recorded 398 PASS and one tst-setvbuf2 failure before timing out;
that failure also reproduced without the patch.  A subsequent xcheck
run had three failures involving installation or privileges.  Other
ordinary-suite failures in that build have not been classified against
a matching baseline.  Other architectures have not been tested.
